# id  tag  kind  t_s  alt_km  L_si_W  flag  note
1 - first_light 0.0030 100.000 2.2218e+04 info  L_si first exceeds 1e-6 of its peak. This is just a visibility threshold; the real value depends on your sensor floor and on where the simulation starts (zstart).
2 A ablation_onset 1.3620 87.381 2.1596e+05 stylized  Net heating flux first exceeds radiative loss at T_ablate = 2300 K, so ablation switches on (a kink in the slope). Real onset is gradual over a few km. BELOW this point the model's light is drag work only (no ablated material): treat light above ~87 km as low-confidence. Loosely like the end of Phase 1 / start of Phase 2 in [R22], but the model has no spin, volatile (Na/K) emission or melt physics.
3 F flare 1.3858 87.161 2.4526e+05 stylized  Light rises x1.4 in 0.07 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: no breakup within 0.05 s (growth of existing pancakes). Dust groups supply 0% of the rise and 0% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
4 Y first_yield 2.6190 75.795 4.2513e+06 physical  Loading rho v^2 reaches S0 = 0.017 MPa. The body starts to flatten (pancake): area, drag and heating all grow, so the light rises faster. Analogue of the onset of fragmentation (Phase 5 / H_f in [R22]).
5 F flare 2.7209 74.859 1.1864e+07 stylized  Light rises x7.9 in 0.26 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: yield gen 0 at 75.8 km (100% of m0); split gen 0 at 74.1 km (100% of m0). Dust groups supply 47% of the rise and 41% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
6 B first_breakup 2.8050 74.088 2.3681e+07 stylized  Spread factor reached f_split = 2: the body breaks into 4 children plus 20 % of its mass as dust. The dust has a large area-to-mass ratio, decelerates and ablates within a fraction of a second, which makes a sharp flare. The instantaneous release is a model simplification. Light grew x5.6 between first yield and this breakup (pancake spreading).
7 F flare 6.3095 43.093 1.0122e+09 stylized  Light rises x2.7 in 0.10 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with: yield gen 1 at 43.1 km (25% of m0); yield gen 1 at 43.1 km (12% of m0); yield gen 1 at 43.1 km (8% of m0) +5 more. Dust groups supply 18% of the rise and 17% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic cascade.
8 P peak 6.3272 42.924 1.6819e+09 physical  Maximum light. It sits ~+0.7 km from the peak of energy deposition (42.2 km) because the luminous efficiency tau(v) falls as the fragments slow (mass-weighted speed here 19.3 km/s).
9 D rolloff 6.6242 40.765 6.1731e+08 physical  Light has fallen to 1/e of the peak 0.30 s after it. Cause: the kinetic energy is being used up (speed 76% of v0, 36% of the mass still in flight) and tau(v) drops with speed. Analogue of Phase 7 (decline until dark flight) in [R22].
10 - drag_overtakes_ablation 6.8822 39.189 2.4033e+08 stylized  Drag work now exceeds the kinetic energy carried off by ablated mass. The ratio ablation/drag is about Lambda v^2/(2 Q Cd), so this happens when the speed falls below ~14 km/s (from the model's heating law).
11 T tail 8.4902 32.918 1.6698e+06 low-confidence  Below 1e-3 of the peak the light is controlled by groups being retired when they slow below v_dark = 3.0 km/s and by the tau taper (2-4 km/s). Do not interpret structure below this level.
